{"step_index":1511,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:55:50Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:55:50+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:55:50+02:00\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\n<truncated 6 lines>\n{\"step_index\":1539,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T23:55:20Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Bounding Roman Dominating Functions**\\n\\nConsidering the goal of bounding the graph radius plus one by the roman weight of any Roman dominating function, the focus is to determine if a proof can be formulated. The question involves exploring the properties of Roman dominating functions to establish a relationship between the graph's radius and the roman weight. The behavior of $B_k(v)$ (ball of radius k centered at v) is also being considered. Specifically, the relationship `card_ballWithin_succ_of_ne_univ` (cardinality of this ball) is relevant if the ball is not the entire universe.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Ball Growth and Structure**\\n\\nThe ball around any vertex grows by at least one at each step, until it encompasses the entire graph. Considering the vertices with function values of 0, 1, and 2, it is deduced that the vertices with a value of 0 are adjacent to some vertex with value 2. Further analysis involves relating the union of balls around vertices with value 2 to the rest of the graph, and deducing that the cardinality of the graph minus this union is less than or equal to the number of vertices with value 1. Now examining the distance between the vertices with value 2 and their relationships with a central vertex.\\n\\n\\n**Examining Layer Intersections via Balls**\\n\\nFocusing on the layers derived from the radius, the observation is that a vertex with a value of 2 ($z$) within layer $j$ can only have neighbors in layers $j-1$, $j$, and $j+1$. This is because the distance to the center $c$ dictates the layer, and any neighbor of $z$ is at most one layer away, based on the definition of $L_k$ and the triangle inequality. Consequently, the ball of radius 1 centered at $z$ can intersect at most three layers.\\n\\n\\n**Relating Layers to Domination**\\n\\nVe\n<truncated 4125 bytes>\n3a9' depends on axioms: [propext, sorryAx, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]\\r\\n'refute_e20263a9' depends on axioms: [propext, sorryAx, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]\\r\\nCHECK: elaborated. Allowed axioms: propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.\\r\\n       'sorryAx' in a list above means that theorem is unfinished.\\r\\n\\n\\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\"}\r\n{\"step_index\":1542,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T23:55:32Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Calculating Maximum Radius for Union**\\n\\nConsidering the case where the vertex set V is the union of closed neighborhoods of two vertices, z1 and z2, which must be connected. The maximum distance between z1 and z2 is at most 3.\\n\\nIf the distance between z1 and z2 is 1, then every vertex in the neighborhood of z1 is at most 1 distance from z1.\\n\\n\\n**Determining Maximum Graph Radius**\\n\\nExamining the cases for distances between z1 and z2, radius of the graph is at most 2 when the distance is 1 or 2, and 3 when the distance is 3. Concluded that whenever the vertex set is the union of closed neighborhoods of two vertices, the radius is no more than 3. This conclusion is verified by considering all possible cases of distance. The maximum radius cannot be greater than 3.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Radius Based on Vertex Sets**\\n\\nConsidered the implications when the set of vertices is not just the union of closed neighborhoods of two vertices.  Introduced a set V1 for vertices outside of these neighborhoods, utilizing this to bound the graph radius.\\n\\nThe case where the distance between z1 and z2 is 1 was explored and concluded that the radius is bounded by W-1. The same bound was found for the distance 2 case. The goal is to compute eccentricity and then radius based on cardinality.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"grep_search\",\"args\":{\"CaseInsensitive\":\"true\",\"MatchPerLine\":\"true\",\"Query\":\"\\\"e20263a9\\\"\",\"SearchPath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Search entire project for conjecture ID\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Grep for e20263a9\\\"\"}}]}\r\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
